Experiment 5: Out of Boundary Paths
Aim

To count the number of ways a ball can move out of an m × n grid in at most maxMove moves, starting from a given cell. The ball can move up, down, left or right.

Algorithm
Make a table dp[r][c] that stores the number of ways to be at cell (r, c). Put 1 at the start cell.
Repeat for each move from 1 to maxMove:
Make an empty table next.
For every cell with dp[r][c] > 0, try all 4 directions.
If the new position is outside the grid, add dp[r][c] to count.
Otherwise, add dp[r][c] to next[newRow][newCol].
Copy next into dp.
Print count (taken modulo 1000000007).
Pseudo code
OutOfBoundary(m, n, maxMove, startRow, startCol)
  dp[startRow][startCol] = 1
  count = 0
  for move = 1 to maxMove
      next = all zeros
      for each cell (r, c)
          for each direction (d)
              nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d]
              if (nr, nc) is outside the grid
                  count = count + dp[r][c]
              else
                  next[nr][nc] = next[nr][nc] + dp[r][c]
      dp = next
  print count
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

#define MOD 1000000007

int main() {
    int m = 2, n = 2, maxMove = 2, startRow = 0, startCol = 0;
    long dp[50][50] = {0};
    long next[50][50];
    int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
    int dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};
    int move, r, c, d, nr, nc;
    long count = 0;

    dp[startRow][startCol] = 1;

    for (move = 0; move < maxMove; move++) {
        for (r = 0; r < m; r++)
            for (c = 0; c < n; c++)
                next[r][c] = 0;

        for (r = 0; r < m; r++) {
            for (c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                if (dp[r][c] == 0)
                    continue;
                for (d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                    nr = r + dr[d];
                    nc = c + dc[d];
                    if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n)
                        count = (count + dp[r][c]) % MOD;
                    else
                        next[nr][nc] = (next[nr][nc] + dp[r][c]) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }

        for (r = 0; r < m; r++)
            for (c = 0; c < n; c++)
                dp[r][c] = next[r][c];
    }

    printf("Number of paths = %ld\n", count);
    return 0;
}
Output
Number of paths = 6
Result

For a 2 × 2 grid with maxMove = 2 and the start at (0, 0), the ball can go out of the boundary in 6 ways.

Time complexity: O(maxMove × m × n)
Space complexity: O(m × n)
